From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felipe Infante de Castro <18445184+fidecastro@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:42:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: x1e80100-microsoft-romulus: Turn off the
 PMIC RTC

The Surface Laptop 7 keeps its time in SAM. At every boot its firmware
recomputes the RTCInfo UEFI offset for the PMK8550 counter from SAM's
clock, so the PMIC RTC is a copy of it, and an offset Linux stores is
discarded at the next boot: after setting the RTC 60 seconds ahead, the
variable held the new offset, and after a reboot it held the one for
SAM's time again.

The firmware writes that offset in UTC and puts the time zone Windows
last set, which SAM also stores, next to it. With "rtc: pm8xxx: honour
the time zone in the UEFI offset" the driver takes the offset for local
time and adds the zone, so a laptop whose Windows was set to UTC-3
started every boot exactly 10800 seconds ahead, until NTP corrected it.
Zeroing the zone in RTCInfo and in RtcTimeSettings did not help: the
firmware restored both at the next boot.

Turn the PMIC RTC off so SAM's clock is rtc0 and sets the system time
at boot, as on the Surface Laptop 8. Setting the time then reaches the
clock the firmware reads.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Felipe Infante de Castro <18445184+fidecastro@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
index 0eee890..772b301 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
@@ -1248,6 +1248,15 @@
 	};
 };
 
+&pmk8550_rtc {
+	/*
+	 * The firmware rewrites this counter's UEFI offset at every boot from
+	 * the clock SAM keeps, so nothing set through it lasts; the time is
+	 * kept by SAM (rtc-surface).
+	 */
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
 &qupv3_0 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
